(from * go #east to #s-palace) (#s-palace is visited)

%% Vignette 1, automatically unlocked
#n-execution
(vignette *)

(narrate vignette for *)
	“On your knees!” You drop down, too scared to resist. Two men in gold-fringed shawls circle around until they’re side by side in front of you. The wiry one keeps his speartip in your face while the stout one pulls out a tablet. (par)
	“Nabuu-ahu-eresh of Babylon, goldsmith of the Temple. For your crimes against His Majesty, Siin-ahhe-eriba, King of the World, King of Assyria, King of the Four Quarters, for your treason and your role in the conspiracy to his murder, you are hereby sentenced to death by impalement.” (par)
	Your breath catches in your throat. “T-treason? Conspiracy? I don’t—what is this—I don’t know what you’re—” (par)
	Your protests are cut off by a warning jab of the spear. You try one more time to force out the words. “I’m innocent! I swear by all the gods! The conspiracy—that wasn’t—my brother and me, we knew about it, but we made a report, he went to Nineveh to tell the palace! He can confirm what I’m saying, we’ll both swear to it in court! Sharhiya of Babylon, he’s a goldsmith too, he’s—” (par)
	The wiry man spits and tosses his spear down in disgust. “Your brother? Oh, he’s told us (emph {plenty}).” He grabs the torch from the wall and holds it up, letting you see the fallen, crumpled shape lying in the shadows. (emph {No...}) (par)
	The stout man looks back at his tablet. “There will be no trial. The sentence is final, by order of Prince Urad-Mullissi.” He turns and strides off into the darkness, while the wiry one looks down at you with a sneer. “Any final words?”
	
	%% A few topics are mentioned
	(unlock #t-babylonia)
	(unlock #t-siin-ahhe-eriba)
	(unlock #t-urad-mullissi)

(protagonist description for *)
	In this memory, you seem to be a tall man in a Babylonian tunic, a little bruised, more than a little dishevelled.

(name *) dungeon
(look *)
	You’re kneeling on hard-packed dirt under a low dirt ceiling. Everything else is darkness outside the little circle of light. (par)
	The (vlink {wiry man}) waves the torch eastward with an awful smirk, letting you see the (vlink {crumpled figure}) lying there. The (vlink {other man}) is mostly lost in the shadows.
	(if) ~(#spear is #in *) (then) %% If the spear *is* here, the appearance rule will replace this paragraph
		(par) The memory of the dropped spear is just a faint afterimage, now that you’ve moved it somewhere else.
	(endif)

(from * go #south to #n-ziqqurat)

(instead of [leave * #east])
	\( going over to the crumpled figure \)
	(try [examine #exec-sharhiya])
(instead of [leave * #north])
	\( going over to the stout man \)
	(try [examine #exec-tzillaya])

#spear
(name *) iron-tipped spear
(an *)
(item *)
(* is #in #n-execution)

(appearance * #in #n-execution)
	The dropped (vlink {spear}) lies just out of arm’s reach.

(descr *)
	(if) (* is #in #n-execution) (then)
		Even if the prisoner couldn’t reach it two thousand years ago, you have no such restrictions—you stand up and walk over to the spear while the scene stays frozen like a diorama around you.
	(endif)
	One and a half meters of well-polished wood, with a sharp iron point mounted on the end. It’s lighter than you’d expected, but feels nice and sturdy.

(suggest pushing *) %% For the door bar puzzle
	(* is #partof $)

#exec-nabiya
(name *) wiry man
(dict *) shawl fringe gold
(male *)
(* is #in #n-execution)
(descr *) Both of the men have fine clothes, richly-dyed and well-fringed, in a way you wouldn’t expect in a dungeon—but this one at least also has strong muscles and callouses on his hands. His (vlink {torch}) is held high, pointing to the east.
(* has secondary objects)

#torch
(name *) torch
(dict *) fire
(fire source *)
(item *)
(* is #heldby #exec-nabiya)
(descr *) The fire looks quite odd, frozen in place, but it still casts enough light to see by.

#exec-sharhiya
(name *) cadaver
(dict *) crumpled figure fallen shape corpse body brother
(* is #in #n-execution)
(descr *)
	(if) (#lamp is in room #n-execution) (fire source #lamp) (then)
		The lamplight lets you make out a bit more detail. Enough to be quite certain he’s dead.
	(else)
		The torchlight \( thankfully \) isn’t bright enough to make out much detail. But you can make out the Babylonian tunic, and the unnatural position.
	(endif)
	(if) (#looseearth is unvisited) (then)
		(par) If this is where “you” died, and the bodies weren’t moved post mortem...then the brother’s bones should be about ten meters to the east, back in the present day.
		(now) (eastern connection unlocked)
	(endif)

#exec-tzillaya
(name *) stout man
(dict *) other
(male *)
(* is #in #n-execution)
(descr *)
	(#lamp is in room #n-execution) (fire source #lamp)
	You can see him better in the lamplight, hurrying away with his tablets—toward some kind of passage to the north. But “you” didn’t see any more than that; this memory can’t tell you what might have been there.
	(now) (northern connection unlocked)
(descr *)
	His form is hazy and hard to make out, just at the edge of your vision. “You”—that is, the person whose memories you’re exploring—must have seen (emph {something}), but the darkness is keeping it hidden from you now.

%% Puzzle room blocking Vignette 2
#n-house
(name *) house
(dict *) entrance hall passage
(room *)
(look *) Or perhaps “entrance hall” would be a better term. The room isn’t large, but the floor is nicely-smoothed clay, the walls painted with abstract (vlink {designs}) in red and blue. By Iron Age standards, whoever lived here must have been distinctly well off. The passage to the west is indistinct, but has a feeling of motion in its haziness.

(from * go #north through #door to #n-ziqqurat)
(from * go #west to #n-announcement)

#house-scenery
(name *) walls and floor
(dict *) wall clay painting design designs
(* is #in #n-house)
(descr *) The walls and floor have been covered with a thin layer of fine white clay, with geometric motifs painted on top once it dried. It looks much nicer than plain mud brick.

#door %% The blocking puzzle itself
(name *) front door
(dict *) wooden wood
(door *)
(openable *)
(descr *)
	The door of the house is made of thick, sturdy wood mounted in the mud-brick walls.
	(if) (* is closed) (then)
		It’s firmly closed, but the (vlink {crack}) where it meets the wall is wide enough to see through.
	(else)
		It’s now hanging open.
	(endif)
(* has secondary objects) (* is closed) %% The crack goes away after that

(prevent [close/lock *])
	(if) (* is open) (then)
		You certainly could close it again, and bar it with the spear—but what would be the point, after all the work it took to open it?
	(else)
		It’s already quite thoroughly shut.
	(endif)

(instead of [put $Obj $Rel *])
	(#crack is #partof *)
	(try [put $Obj $Rel #crack])

(instead of [unlock * with #spear])
	(#crack is #partof *)
	\( putting the spear in the crack \) (line)
	(try [put #spear #in #crack])
	(tick)
	\( pushing the spear \) (line)
	(try [push #spear])

#crack
(name *) wide crack
(dict *) heavy bar of wood
(* is #partof #door)
(container *)
(descr *)
	The crack is about three centimeters wide. Through it you can see a heavy bar of wood on the other side, holding the door closed.
	(if) (#spear is #partof *) (then)
		(par) A (vlink {spear}) is wedged under the bar.
	(endif)
(* has secondary objects) (#spear is #partof *)

(instead of [search *])
	(try [examine *])
(instead of [look $ *])
	(try [examine *])
(instead of [open *])
	(try [open #door])
(instead of [put $Obj $Rel *]) %% In case of PUT SPEAR UNDER BAR
	~($Rel = #in)
	(try [put $Obj #in *])

(prevent [put $Obj #in *])
	~($Obj = #spear)
	The crack is only a few centimeters wide, not large enough to fit (a $Obj) in.

(perform [put #spear #in *])
	You push the spear through the crack, wedging it under the bar.
	(now) (#spear is #partof *)

(perform [push/pull/turn #spear])
	(#spear is #partof *)
	You give the spear a mighty shove...and nothing happens. Another shove, still nothing. The bar is quite firmly wedged in place. But when you set your full weight against it, you feel something start to give—and then with an almighty (emph {CRASH}) the bar is forced aside! The spear clatters to the ground as the door swings open.
	(now) (* is nowhere)
	(now) (#spear is #in #n-ziqqurat)
	(now) (#door is open)

%% Vignette 2
#n-announcement
(vignette *)

(narrate vignette for *)
	“Attention, citizens of Babylon!” A new voice cuts through the mid-morning chatter. You set down your bucket with a sigh, and give your brother a nudge. A stranger has stepped up on the edge of the fountain to bellow to the crowd. (par)
	“His Majesty Siin-ahhe-eriba, Great King, Mighty King, King of the World, King of Assyria, King of the Four Quarters, the capable shepherd, favorite of the Great Gods, guardian of truth, he who loves justice, renders assistance, offers aid to the weak, and strives after good deeds, the Perfect Man, the virile warrior, foremost of all rulers, the bridle that tames the rebellious, the one who strikes his enemies with lightning—” (par)
	He cuts off abruptly, glancing around. The tone of the crowd has become decidedly hostile. He swallows, clears his throat, and starts again. “His Majesty, in his wisdom, has selected an heir. After his passing, he will not be succeeded by his eldest son, Urad-Mullissi, but by his chosen crown prince, Asshur-ahu-iddina.” (par)
	(emph {That}) gets the crowd’s attention. Sharhiya stands up, squinting at the messenger. “Asshur-ahu-iddina? He’s not even a son of the queen, is he?” (par)
	You shake your head. “No. Just a concubine. And he’s (emph {young}), too. But they say his mother is a clever one. Maybe he takes after her.” (par)
	He shushes you, and you go quiet as the messenger continues. “In one month’s time, all the lords of Babylonia will assemble at the Great Palace in Nineveh to renew your oaths of fealty. May His Maj—” (par)
	That’s when a vegetable hits him across the face. “Heir or no heir, he’s no true king until he’s crowned by Marduk!” The messenger tries to get back to his feet, but the whole audience is shouting now. “Send soldiers if you want! What’ll they do that they haven’t done already?” “Make him remember what happened to the last crown prince who set foot in Babylon!” (par)
	“Come on!” Sharhiya grabs you by the shoulder, and the pair of you turn and run. There’s no sign of the messenger now behind the crowd of angry Babylonians.
	(unlock #t-babylonia)
	(unlock #t-gods)
	(unlock #t-siin-ahhe-eriba)
	(unlock #t-urad-mullissi)
	(unlock #t-asshur-ahu-iddina)

(protagonist description for *)
	You’re the same tall man in a Babylonian tunic, though your clothes are simpler and less dishevelled here than at the execution. Daily about-town sort of wear.

(name *) marketplace
(look *)
	This plaza must have been magnificent, at one point. The soot-blackened (vlink {buildings}) soar high, the walls are covered in shattered reliefs, and the (vlink {grand fountain}) shows the remains of some kind of sculpture. (par) All around you, the (vlink {crowd}) is frozen like a sea of nameless, faceless statues.

(from * go #east to #n-house)

#ann-buildings
(name *) soot-blackened buildings
(plural *)
(dict *) soot blackened building wall walls relief reliefs
(* is #in #n-announcement)
(descr *) Some of the buildings have crumbled beyond recognition. Others have been hastily repaired, at least enough to live in. Only a few have been properly rebuilt. Mud brick is pretty resilient against fire, but not against dozens of people going at the walls with chisels and hammers.

#ann-fountain
(name *) grand fountain
(dict *) sculpture statue water mildly stagnant
(* is #in #n-announcement)
(descr *) Whatever this fountain used to be, now it’s just an enormous basin full of mildly stagnant water. Cracks on one side leak a trickle out onto the ground—you don’t see any sort of aqueduct, so it must need regular refilling.
(liquid source *)
(* is filled with #water)

#ann-people
(name *) crowd of people
(* is #in #n-announcement)
(gender-neutral *) %% Uses singular verbs with the noun and plural verbs with the pronoun
(dict *) messenger man assyrian babylonian babylonians
(descr *)
	You have to move around a bit to see the messenger, but he stands out: his dress and his dialect are both Assyrian, while everyone else here is distinctly Babylonian. Fortunately you’re proficient in both. \( A necromancy degree involves quite a lot of dead languages. \) (par)
	The only other individual you can make out is your (vlink {brother}), caught mid-run with both feet off the ground. The rest are remembered only as a sort of indistinct blur.
(* has secondary objects)

#ann-sharhiya
(name *) brother
(your *)
(dict *) sharhiya
(* is #in #n-announcement)
(male *)
(descr *) Noticeably shorter than you, and just a bit rounder, but the family resemblance is easy to see.

#bucket
(name *)
	(if) (* is pristine) (then) dropped (end if)
	bucket
(item *)
(* is #in #n-announcement)
(descr *) It’s made of leather instead of plastic, but the best way to carry water hasn’t really changed in the past two thousand years.
(liquid container *)
(initial appearance *) Your \( well, “your” \) bucket has fallen next to the fountain, abandoned in your haste.

%% Puzzle room blocking Vignette 3
#n-workshop
(room *)
(name *) workshop
(dict *) work shop
(look *) This room is not small: the ceiling is high and the floor wide. But the heat and the smoke make it feel tight all the same, and you’re hemmed in on all sides by (vlink {tools and equipment}). The scent of hot metal stings in your nose.

(* attracts #light) %% Keep the light here even when it’s not a door

(from * go #east through #light to #n-ziqqurat) ~(statue is pushed)
(from * go #east to #n-ziqqurat) (statue is pushed)
(from * go #southwest to #n-argument)

#tools
(name *) tools and equipment
(plural *)
(dict *) tool crucibles crucible anvils anvil hammers hammer
(* is #in #n-workshop)
(descr *) Crucibles, anvils, hammers, all sorts of tools you don’t even know the names of, blurring and merging into a sort of formless collective every time you take your eyes off them. The (vlink {forge}) is big enough to stay mostly in one place, at least, and pieces of (vlink {gold and silver}) shine in the light.
(* has secondary objects)
(item *) %% Not really
(prevent [take *]) %% But it gives some lore on how memories work
	You try to pick up one of the tools, but you can’t pull it free of the rest. Without a goldsmith’s knowledge, these aren’t really individual objects in your mind, just as a vague, collective mass of equipment.

#forge
(name *) forge
(dict *) coal coals hot heat wave waves
(* is #partof #tools)
(descr *) It’s about the size of a microwave, probably better for fine detail work than large-scale blacksmithing. The heat waves from the hot coals are frozen in memory, making the air look like an obscure-glass window.
(fire source *)

%% The puzzle itself
#light
(name *) blinding light
(dict *) beam
(door *) %% Technically
(intangible *)
~(action [enter *] requires * to be touched) %% You don’t have to touch it to enter it
(when * blocks passage)
	~(current room #workshop) %% One-way door
	~(worn #blindfold)
	You squint your eyes and try to move through the light, but it’s like pushing against a strong wind: it burns in your eyes, physically forcing you back. Eventually you’re forced to retreat, tears streaming down your face.
(when * blocks passage)
	(current room #workshop)
	(current player $Player)
	~(#blindfold has ancestor $Player)
	It wouldn’t be hard to move through the light in this direction, but if you left the blindfold behind, how would you ever get back?
	(if) (player can see #blindfold) (then)
		Better (link [take the blindfold]) {take it with you}.
	(endif)
(descr *) It’s a focused, directed ray that almost reminds you of a laser, or a sunbeam shining through dust. From the side you can clearly see its path traced out in fiery gold.

#statue
(name *) statue of Marduk
(dict *) cult gold-plated gold plated skin
(heads *) statue Marduk
(suggest pushing *)
(* is #in #n-workshop)
(* has secondary objects)

(appearance *)
	A towering (vlink {statue}) dominates the room, light blazing from its gold-plated skin.
	(if) ~(statue is pushed) (then)
		Its fiery gaze is fixed on the door to the east.
	(else)
		The work platform has been rotated so it now faces the north wall.
	(endif)

(descr *)
	It stands tall and imperious, at least double your height, its skin plated with finely-hammered gold and silver, its hair and beard set with lapis lazuli. It must represent one of the gods, probably Marduk; a fiery glow radiates from its face, filling
	(if) (statue is pushed) (then)
		the north side of the room
	(else)
		the eastern passage
	(endif)
	with light. (par)
	It’s only once your eyes adjust that you can see the more mundane side: the wooden scaffolding around it, the movable (vlink {work platform}) underneath. This god seems to be under construction.
	(unlock #t-gods)

#st-platform
(name *) work platform
(dict *) movable scaffolding scaffold wooden wood
(suggest pushing *)
(* is #partof #statue)
(descr *) The whole apparatus seems designed to rotate, letting the goldsmiths access every side of their work.

(instead of [push/pull/turn #statue | $])
	(try [push *])
(perform [push/pull/turn * | $])
	(if) ~(statue is pushed) (then)
		Squinting against the glare, you take hold of the platform—(no space) (emph {not}) touching the statue itself—and give it a mighty heave. It shakes and rocks, tilting dangerously to one side for a moment...but then it starts to turn. Marduk’s gaze burns a fiery trail across the wall until it’s aimed safely away from the doors.
		(now) (statue is pushed)
	(else)
		You adjust the position of the statue a bit, making sure the gaze doesn’t linger too long on any one spot.
	(endif)

%% Vignette 3
#n-argument
(vignette *)

(narrate vignette for *)
	“Absolutely not.” You glare across the table at your brother. The single lamp gives his face a strange, uncanny cast. “We have everything to lose, and (emph {nothing}) to gain.” (par)
	“Except for saving someone’s life. Saving the (emph {king’s}) life!” (par)
	“He wasn’t crowned by Marduk. He’s no true king. And have you forgotten what he did to Babylon? Let the Assyrians all stab each other in the back, for all I care. What happens up in Nineveh isn’t our concern.” (par)
	“If some Babylonians save the king’s life, maybe that will change.” He searches your face, eyes gleaming, and sighs at what he sees. “And besides. The crown prince is different. Asshur-ahu-iddina spends time around here, he fixes things that need fixing. Urad-Mullissi is just another Assyrian. If he seizes the throne, this continues on for decades. If we stop this, if Asshur-ahu-iddina inherits, we’ll have someone reasonable in power. Someone who will get Marduk’s blessing and rebuild the temples.” (par)
	You close your eyes for a long moment. “Fine. Make the trip, make your report. I’ll take care of things back here. But if you expect gratitude from the Assyrians...” (par)
	You meet his gaze and hold it as the shadows start to close in. “You’re going to be disappointed.” (par)
	And the light gutters out.

(name *) back room
(dict *) backroom
(look *)
	This room is suggested more than seen, the moonlight through the (vlink {window}) tracing the outlines in shades of black.
	(if) (#lamp is in room *) (fire source #lamp) (then)
		Even the lamplight goes soft and silvery in the memory.
	(endif)
	Is it a workshop, a kitchen, a storage space? All you can see clearly is (vlink {your brother}), eyes fixed intently on the (vlink {table}).

(from * go #northeast to #n-workshop)

